**Read:** [Contamination control engineering — Tribble and colleagues, NASA CR 4740](../sources/Contamination-Control-Engineering-NASA.lingua#r_2XKKPYTW2DPQPQZ8VPV23BJ8A5), definitions; [Calibration of aerosol instruments — Chen, Fletcher and Cheng](../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP), section 21.2. Use the shared arithmetic and units reading as needed.

A millimetre is 10⁻³ m, a micrometre 10⁻⁶ m and a nanometre 10⁻⁹ m. Squaring or cubing a length conversion also squares or cubes its factor. A particle's relevant size can be comparable to a gap, coating or pattern even when it is too small to see unaided.

Size alone does not identify material or effect. A hard fragment, soft droplet and biological aggregate with the same nominal diameter can behave differently. State both the dimensional comparison and what physical behavior you expect from it.

**Work:** Draw a scale from metres to nanometres and place your object's dimensions on it. Convert 0.5 µm into metres and nanometres, and 1 cm² into square metres. Compare a hypothetical 10 µm particle with a 20 µm gap without assuming that every such particle will enter, lodge or damage the gap.

